{"entities":[{"pmid":"458486","text":"Tiapride","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D063325","start":0,"end":8},{"pmid":"458486","text":"levodopa","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D007980","start":12,"end":20},{"pmid":"458486","text":"involuntary movements","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D004409","start":29,"end":50},{"pmid":"458486","text":"Tiapride","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D063325","start":52,"end":60},{"pmid":"458486","text":"benzamide","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"C037689","start":76,"end":85},{"pmid":"458486","text":"metoclopramide","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D008787","start":116,"end":130},{"pmid":"458486","text":"levodopa","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D007980","start":140,"end":148},{"pmid":"458486","text":"involuntary movements","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D004409","start":167,"end":188},{"pmid":"458486","text":"idiopathic Parkinson's disease","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D010300","start":209,"end":239},{"pmid":"458486","text":"Parkinsonism","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D010300","start":294,"end":306},{"pmid":"458486","text":"akinesia","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D004409","start":339,"end":347},{"pmid":"458486","text":"Tiapride","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D063325","start":385,"end":393},{"pmid":"458486","text":"levodopa","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D007980","start":411,"end":419},{"pmid":"458486","text":"dystonia","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D004421","start":468,"end":476},{"pmid":"458486","text":"levodopa","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D007980","start":524,"end":532},{"pmid":"458486","text":"dyskinesias","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D004409","start":541,"end":552},{"pmid":"458486","text":"dopamine","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D004298","start":606,"end":614}],"gold_relations":"levodopa | CID | dyskinesias","metrics":{"precision":0.14285714285714285,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.25,"tp":1,"fp":6,"fn":0,"rel_precision":0.14285714285714285,"rel_recall":1.0,"rel_f1":0.25,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":6,"rel_fn":0},"pmid":"458486","relations":["D007980 | CID | D004409","D007980 | CID | D004421","D063325 | CID | C537921","D063325 | CID | D020820","D007980 | CID | D020820","D007980 | CID | D063325","D063325 | CID | D010300"],"text":"Tiapride in levodopa-induced involuntary movements. Tiapride, a substituted benzamide derivative closely related to metoclopramide, reduced levodopa-induced peak dose involuntary movements in 16 patients with idiopathic Parkinson's disease. However, an unacceptable increase in disability from Parkinsonism with aggravation of end-of-dose akinesia led to its cessation in 14 patients. Tiapride had no effect on levodopa-induced early morning of \"off-period\" segmental dystonia. These results fail to support the notion that levodopa-induced dyskinesias are caused by overstimulation of a separate group of dopamine receptors."}